---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SubjectGallery from '../../components/SubjectGallery.astro';

const title = 'Vehicle Papercraft Designs: Cars, Bikes & Armor';
const description = 'Free AI vehicle papercraft designs — classic cars, muscle cars, motorcycles and armored vehicles. Open a design, copy the prompt, generate your own.';

const siblings = [
  { href: '/gallery/animal/', label: 'Animals' },
  { href: '/gallery/dragon/', label: 'Dragons' },
  { href: '/gallery/vehicle/', label: 'Vehicles' },
  { href: '/gallery/building/', label: 'Buildings' },
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} ogType="website">
  <section class="hero container" style="padding-bottom:10px">
    <nav class="breadcrumb" style="margin-bottom:16px">
      <a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/gallery/">Gallery</a><span>/</span><span>Vehicles</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="reveal">Vehicle Papercraft Designs</h1>
    <p class="reveal" style="max-width:720px">
      Vehicles are a test of straight lines. Bodies are made of long flat panels, so a wobbly crease shows
      immediately, and the silhouette carries almost all of the recognition — a muscle car reads as a muscle
      car from the roofline alone. That makes vehicles the best subject for practicing clean scoring and
      crisp folds.
    </p>
    <p class="reveal" style="max-width:720px">
      The designs below run from classic and muscle cars to motorcycles and armored transports. Open any one
      for its prompt, then swap the body style, color or era to get something different. Heavy cardstock
      (180–220 gsm) suits vehicles better than thin paper because the panels need to hold themselves flat
      without glue pulling them into a curve — see
      <a href="/learn/what-paper-for-origami/">which paper to use</a> for the full weight comparison.
    </p>
  </section>

  <SubjectGallery subject="vehicle" heading="Vehicle papercraft designs" intro="" siblings={siblings} />
</BaseLayout>
